               SENATE COMMITTEE ON EDUCATION
                     Dede Alpert, Chair
           1999-2000 First Extraordinary Session
                              

BILL NO:       SB2x
AUTHOR:        O'Connell
AMENDED:       As proposed to be amended
FISCAL COMM.:  Yes                      HEARING DATE:  
February 10, 1999
URGENCY:       No             CONSULTANT: James Wilson
 <u> 
SPECIAL NOTE
 </u> 
This bill was previously heard at the February 3, 1999  
hearing of this Committee, with no action taken at that  
time.  Amendments have been proposed that are reflected in  
this Analysis.
 <u> 
SUMMARY </u> 

This bill requires the Superintendent of Public Instruction  
to begin to develop a high school exit examination covering  
reading, writing and mathematics.  The bill further  
requires that the State Board of Education adopt an exit  
exam by October 1, 2000, while 12th grade students in the  
2002-2003 school year will be the first class required to  
pass the test in order to receive a diploma. 

 <u> BACKGROUND </u> 

Currently, there are two statewide testing requirements  
concerning schools: 

 <u> STAR (Standardized Testing and Reporting) </u> (SB 376 /1997).   
All students in grades 2 through 11 are tested in the  
Spring of each year on a standardized, commercially  
published ("off-the-shelf"), test of basic skills that is  
designated by the State Board of Education.  The Board has  
currently designated the Stanford Achievement Test, form 9,  
(SAT9 or Stanford 9) test published by Harcourt Brace.   
This test provides individual student scores that are  
cross-referenced to the publisher's national test taking  
population ("national norm"),

 <u> Local Proficiency Tests </u> (AB 65 /1977).  All school  




                                                        SB2x
                                                      Page 2



districts maintaining any of the grades 6 through 12 are  
required to establish basic skills proficiency standards  
and administer proficiency assessments (usually tests) that  
all students must pass in order to graduate.  Tests and  
standards are locally developed and aligned to local  
curriculum, provided that they address, at a minimum,  
"reading comprehension, writing and computational skills,  
in the English language".  Different standards and testing  
procedures are authorized for special education pupils and  
other pupils with a diagnosed learning disability.   
Assessment of pupil proficiency is required at least once  
each during grades 4 through 6, and 7 through 9, and twice  
during grades 10 and 11.  Pupils of limited English  
proficiency (LEP) are not tested until they have received  
at least 24 months of instruction, including six months  
instruction in English.
 <u> 
Current Graduation Requirements </u> <strike> </strike> <u> . </u>  Current law requires  
California students to complete state and locally specified  
courses in order to graduate from high school.  Although  
attainment of local proficiency standards (described above)  
is required, there is currently no single statewide test  
required for high school graduation.
 <u> 
Academic Standards </u> <strike> </strike> <u> . </u>  Current law calls for the State Board  
of Education to adopt content and performance standards,  
but does not mandate the adoption of those standards  
locally.  To date, the Board has adopted content standards  
for subjects and grade levels specified in statute.  None  
of the current tests is "aligned" to the state standards,  
although the <strike> </strike> STAR <strike> </strike> test will be augmented with the  
addition of standards based questions when it is  
administered in spring 1999. 

 <u> Statewide Pupil Assessment Review (SPAR) </u> .  Current law  
calls for appointment of a six-member test review panel, a  
majority of whom are to be parents of K-12 public school  
children.  This panel is charged with reviewing statewide  
tests to ensure that test questions do not invite  
disclosure of personal beliefs or practices.

 <u> Property Rights and Test Design. </u>  In a lawsuit (Debra P. v.  
Turlington) that challenged the state of Florida over their  
exit exam, a federal Court of Appeals determined that  
students have a legal property interest in receiving a  
diploma (thus the state must have a legally defensible  




                                                        SB2x
                                                      Page 3



position if it denies a diploma to an otherwise qualified  
person).  The court laid out three principles that should  
be considered for high school exit exams.

1.The test must not intentionally discriminate against a  
  protected group or class.

2.A graduation test must be a fair measure of what students  
  have been taught, that is, it must have "curricular  
  validity".

3.Students must have adequate advance notice of test  
  requirements.

 <u> ANALYSIS </u> 
 <u> 
This bill </u> :

1)   Requires the Superintendent of Public Instruction  
     (SPI), with the approval of the State Board of  
     Education (SBE), to develop a high school exit exam in  
     language arts and mathematics in accordance with the  
     State Board adopted academically rigorous content  
     standards. .

 <u> 2)   R </u> <strike>By July 1, 2000, r </strike> equires the State Board of  
     Education to adopt a high school exit examination that  
     is aligned to state standards by October 1, 2000.

3)   




















                                                        SB2x
                                                      Page 4



Requires that all students in the graduating class of  
     2002-2003, and all later years, pass the exit  
     examination in order to receive a diploma of high  
     school graduation. 

4)   Provides for the SPI to appoint a High School Exit  
     Examination Panel to assist with the development of  
     the test.  The Panel is comprised of teachers,  
     administrators, school board members, and the general  
     public.

5)   Requires that the exit examination be field tested  
     prior to implementation.

6)   Requires that the exit examination be reviewed by the  
     Statewide Pupil Assessment Review (SPAR) Panel to  
     ensure that no questions elicit information concerning  
     personal beliefs or practices.

7)   Requires that the high school exit exam meet standards  
     (similar to those set forth in Debra P. v.  
     Turlington), including: 

     a)        Tests are not to be administered to pupils  
          who have not received adequate notice, defined to  
          be written notice in the 9th grade or upon  
          transfer into the district.
     b)        Tests may not intentionally discriminate on  
          the basis of race, gender or national origin.
     c)        Tests shall have instructional validity,  
          defined to mean the test is "consistent" with  
          what is taught to the pupil; and curricular  
          validity, defined to reflect the content of  
          textbooks.

8)   Provides for accommodation of special education  
     students by requiring the development of differential  
     standards and assessment procedures.  This is similar  
     to existing accommodations provided for special needs  
     pupils who are being assessed to local proficiency  
     standards.

9)   Requires that each pupil take the exit exam in grade  
     ten and be allowed to take the exam at all subsequent  
     administrations until each part of the exam has been  
     passed.  Pupils are also allowed, but not required, to  




                                                        SB2x
                                                      Page 5



     take the test in grade nine.

10)  Allows districts to defer testing of English Language  
     Learners (ELL) until the pupil has been in California  
     schools for two years, during which time the ELL pupil  
     should have received at least six months of  
     instruction in reading, writing and comprehension in  
     the English language.  Deferring test administration  
     does not exempt the pupil from taking the exit test,  
     and the pupil must still pass the test in English in  
     order to graduate.

11)  Requires that supplemental instruction be provided to  
     any pupil who "does not demonstrate sufficient  
     progress toward passing the exit examination."   
     Further authorizes school districts to use funds  
     allocated for after school programs, tutoring programs  
     and remedial summer school for supplemental  
     instruction.

12)  Finds that:

     a)        Local proficiency standards are set too low  
          and are not consistent with state standards.
     b)        The high school exit exam should set high <strike> er </strike>  
          expectations for graduation and basic skills  
          proficiency.

13)  Repeals existing requirements for establishment of  
     local proficiency standards and assessment of pupils  
     to those standards. (Repeals Article 2.5 of Chapter 2  
     of Part 28 of the Education Code, commencing with  
     Section 51215.)
 
14)  Appropriates two million dollars ($2,000,000) of  
     federal Goals 2000 funding to the State Superintendent  
     of Public Instruction to develop the high school exit  
     examination.

 <u> STAFF COMMENTS </u> 

 <u> 1)   Aligning curriculum to standards and tests to  
     curriculum. </u>  The state has provided for the  
     establishment of academic standards, although the  
     state has not required that local school districts  
     align their curriculum accordingly.  In order to  




                                                        SB2x
                                                      Page 6



     encourage the local adoption of a standards based  
     curriculum, the state has already set about to revise  
     instructional materials, and this bill would provide  
     another incentive by establishing a high school exit  
     examination that is aligned to the standards and has  
     curricular validity.  In order to provide their  
     students with a fair opportunity to pass the exit  
     test, school districts will be forced to align their  
     curriculum to the "voluntary" standards

The exit exam will be given to students in the tenth grade,  
     In order to know how their students are progressing,  
     districts will also need to test pupils in primary and  
     middle schools using a test that has similar  
     curricular validity as the exit exam.  Since the STAR  
     test is given to pupils in each grade two through 11,  
     it would be the obvious choice to use for the purpose  
     of tracking student progress.  Unfortunately, the STAR  
     exam is a generic test of basic skills that does not  
     necessarily have "curricular validity" as yet.   The  
     STAR test is being augmented with standards based  
     items, but the basic test is still not assured to  
     match the state standards, and therefore would not be  
     "aligned" to the standards based exit exam.

Staff recommends that the STAR tests be revised in future  
     years to align to the curriculum, which in turn should  
     be aligned to the state's academic standards.   If the  
     STAR and exit tests can be rationalized and validated  
     to the same curricular standards, then the articulated  
     STAR testing can be used in grades two through nine as  
     an early warning system for the exit exam.

2)   
















                                                        SB2x
                                                      Page 7



 <u> Test failure and diagnosing learning disabilities. </u>  A  
     sizable percentage of students who fail the exit exam,  
     or any academic test, may do so because they suffer  
     from an undiagnosed learning disabilities that prevent  
     them from processing information in a "normal"  
     fashion.  Dyslexia is one of the better known learning  
     disabilities, but there are many others that prevent  
     the individual from reading or otherwise processing  
     information.  Providing remedial instruction to these  
     pupils is unlikely to remedy their problem, although  
     special education can often be provided that will help  
     the individual cope with, or otherwise overcome, their  
     learning disorder.  Some experts say that the tenth  
     grade, when the exit test may first be administered,  
     is too late to address some learning disabilies.   
     Therefore, staff recommends that the bill be amended  
     to require that any pupil who fails the exit  
     examination, or is deemed likely to fail based upon  
     results of STAR testing in any of the grades two  
     through 11, be assessed by qualified staff to  
     determine if a learning disability may need correction  
     or accommodation.

 <u> 3)   Unintended consequence. </u>  Currently school districts  
     are entitled to state reimbursement at specified  
     hourly rates for remedial summer school (or after  
     school or Saturday school) for pupils in grades seven  
     through 12 who fail, or are at risk of failing, local  
     proficiency assessments.  By repealing the code  
     sections that require local proficiency assessments,  
     this bill inadvertently repeals the basis for the  
     grade seven through 12 "proficiency summer school."   
     Staff recommends that Education Code Section 37252 be  
     amended to continue to provide reimbursement (at  
     prescribed hourly rates) to school districts that  
     provide remedial summer school, after school and  
     Saturday instruction to pupils who fail, or are at  
     risk of failing, the high school exit exam. 

 <u> 4)   Will the test withstand legal challenge? </u>  The bill has  
     been amended to require that the test meet three  
     standards similar to those set forth in the Florida  
     case (Debra P. v. Turlington) but it is not clear that  
     this will be sufficient to withstand court challenges.  
      For example, the requirement to provide "notice" to  
     pupils may require that pupils understand the skills  




                                                        SB2x
                                                      Page 8



     and knowledge that will be required to pass the test,  
     and not just that a test will be required for  
     graduation.  The bill calls for the State Board to  
     adopt the exit exam by October 1, 2000, when the first  
     class required to pass the exam will be entering 10th  
     grade.  Therefore, the first class that must pass the  
     test will have only a few months notice of the content  
     of the adopted exam before they will be attempting it  
     for the first time. 
 
 <u> 5)   Should the test be used in a "compensatory model" of  
     graduation requirements? </u>  As currently drafted, this  
     bill presumes that passage of the exit test by a  
     certain "cut off" score will be an absolute  
     requirement for high school graduation.  The bill  
     provides no alternative means for students without  
     special needs to meet the new standards.  Such an  
     absolute requirement may be exceedingly difficult to  
     defend since it implies that a score that is one point  
     above the "cut off" is fully qualified and a score  
     that is one point below the "cut off" is unqualified.   
     It may be more realistic to set a range of qualifying  
     scores so that performance on other measures (such as  
     grades) might compensate for poor test performance, or  
     the reverse.

Such a "compensatory model" is used by the University of  
     California and the California State University for  
     admissions.  In these instances, a high test score on  
     the SAT or ACT tests may be used to compensate for a  
     lower grade point average (GPA).  Conversely, a high  
     GPA can compensate for a lower test score.  

Staff recommends that the Superintendent of Public  
     Instruction and the State Board of Education be given  
     direction in the bill to explore how the exit test  
     might be used as part of a compensatory graduation  
     requirement.

 <u> 6)   Use End of Course Exams to Test Higher Order Skills. </u>    
     Both New York and Texas give students the choice of  
     end-of-course exams in lieu of a single exit exam.   
     There are advantages to using end-of-course exams in  
     this way.  For one, curricular validity is assured  
     because the students have to take the course before  
     the exam.  Another advantage is that students can take  




                                                        SB2x
                                                      Page 9



     a selection of courses and exams that reflects their  
     talents and strengths and avoid subjects in which they  
     lack talent and ability.  A series of end-of-course  
     exams also allows flexibility to accommodate some  
     special needs and spreads out the testing over several  
     years, and subjects, instead of "staking all" on one  
     test. 
Staff recommends that the Superintendent and State Board be  
     directed to explore the use of end-of-course exams in  
     a selection of advanced courses that could be combined  
     with basic exit examination in order to add rigor, and  
     offer alternatives, to graduation testing requirements  
     in future years.  The use of these tests should only  
     be considered in connection with a "compensatory"  
     requirement model as recommended.

 <u> 7)   Appropriate fund source? </u>  The last time the state used  
     federal funds to develop a state mandated test,  
     federal auditors charged the state with supplanting  
     and the state was forced to repay the federal funds.   
     The fund source in that case was Chapter 2 block grant  
     funding, which is generally considered to be the  
     predecessor to Goals 2000 funding.  The "supplanting"  
     activity was development of California Assessment  
     Program (CAP) testing.  Given the similarities between  
     the historical audit exception, and the proposed  
     appropriation, staff recommends that the appropriation  
     be made from state general, rather than federal,  
     funds.

 <u> SUPPORT </u> 

American Federation of State, County and Municipal  
Employees (AFSCME)
California Manufacturers Association

 <u> OPPOSITION </u> 

None received <u> 
